Below is a small but complete C++ program that shows the essentials of working with `std::thread`:

```cpp
/*
  Thread demo
  ------------
  • Launches several threads.
  • Each thread increments a shared counter a few times.
  • Mutex protects the counter from data races.
  • We print the thread id and the counter value.
  • All threads are joined before the program exits.
*/

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>
#include <vector>

// Shared data ---------------------------------------------------------------

int counter = 0;            // The counter that all threads will modify.
std::mutex counter_mutex;   // Protects access to 'counter'.

// Thread worker --------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  worker() – a function that will run in its own thread.

  Parameters:
    id      – an identifier that we print to distinguish the threads.
    loops   – how many times the thread should increment the counter.
*/
void worker(int id, int loops)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < loops; ++i)
    {
        // Simulate some work:
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(50));

        // Lock the mutex before touching the shared counter.
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(counter_mutex);
            ++counter;
            std::cout << "Thread " << id
                      << " (id=" << std::this_thread::get_id()
                      << ") incremented counter to " << counter << '\n';
        }
    }
}

// Main ------------------------------------------------------------------------

int main()
{
    std::cout << "Main thread (id=" << std::this_thread::get_id() << ")\n";

    // Create a few threads that run 'worker'.
    const int num_threads = 3;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; ++i)
    {
        // Pass the thread number and how many increments each should perform.
        threads.emplace_back(worker, i + 1, 10);
    }

    // Wait for all threads to finish.
    for (auto &t : threads)
    {
        if (t.joinable())
            t.join();
    }

    std::cout << "All threads finished. Final counter = " << counter << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

### How to compile & run

```bash
# g++ (GNU C++ compiler) – needs C++11 or newer
g++ -std=c++17 -pthread -O2 -o thread_demo thread_demo.cpp

# run
./thread_demo
```

*(On Windows you may need to add `/std:c++17` and ensure the Visual C++ toolchain is used.)*

### What the program shows

| Feature | Code snippet |
|---------|--------------|
| **Thread creation** | `threads.emplace_back(worker, i + 1, 10);` |
| **Passing arguments** | `worker(int id, int loops)` |
| **Thread ID** | `std::this_thread::get_id()` |
| **Synchronization** | `std::mutex counter_mutex` & `std::lock_guard` |
| **Joining threads** | `t.join();` |
| **Shared state** | global `int counter` |

You can modify the program to experiment with `detach()`, `async`, or more complex synchronization primitives (condition variables, barriers, etc.). The example above should give you a solid starting point for working with `std::thread`.
